  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Active weather today through tonight, followed by \nperiodic chances for showers and storms through midweek, although \nplenty of dry time can be expected.\n\nActive weather will return for today and tonight as high pressure \nexits to our east. This will initially allow a warm frontal segment \nto drift northeast across the region this morning in response to a \nmid/upper shortwave dropping into the western Great Lakes. This will \nbring the next chance for showers and isolated storms into western \nNew York early this morning, possibly impacting the morning commute. \nThis initial batch of showers and isolated storms will then shift \neast across the remainder of the area through midday/early afternoon.\n\nOverall environment does a look a bit more favorable for a few \nstronger storms across areas south of Lake Ontario this afternoon \nand evening. Moderate shear and deep moisture (PWAT values in the \n1.5\"-1.75\" range) will already be in place, so as the atmosphere \ndestabilizes this afternoon, this will bring the better chance for \nsome more robust scattered convection. The main focus for storms \nfrom mid afternoon into this evening will be along lake breeze \nconvergence boundaries south of Lake Ontario and east of Lake Erie.\n\nA cold front will then push south across western and north-central \nNew York late this evening through the early overnight. Moderate \nshear and a moist airmass will remain in place within the warm \nsector ahead of the boundary, however loss of daytime heating will \nresult in less instability. That said, as lake breezes flip to land \nbreezes and move back out over the warm lake waters, can't rule out \na couple stronger storms possibly forming along and just ahead of \nthe surface cold front as it interacts with any lingering \nboundaries, especially over the lakes. \n\nAs mentioned above, the threat for a few severe storms has been \nexpanded northward with the latest update from SPC, with the main \nthreat continuing to be strong winds. Thus, a Marginal Risk of \nSevere Weather (level 1 of 5) now includes all areas south of Lake \nOntario for mid afternoon through the early overnight. Potential for \nlocalized heavy rainfall remains in place within the moist airmass, \nfor which WPC continues with a Marginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall \n(level 1 of 4) for the entire area. \n\nThe cold front will stall just to our south tonight, then remain \nnearly stationary through midweek. Drier air will reside to the \nnorth of the boundary, with the main threat of showers and a few \nstorms toward the western Southern Tier Tuesday through Wednesday \ncloser to the frontal boundary, and mainly dry weather elsewhere.",
